A||||6||Posthumane||HP vs. 1/4 mile times||||1||
Z||000000||Posthumane||08-24-2001||04:24 PM||||I was reading a car and driver mag yesterday and there was a couple of formulas in there for calculating horsepower from your quarter mile times or end speed based on vehicle weight. I tried this with my duke and got about 70 HP from both formulas using a vehicle weight of 2600 lbs. As far as I know, the duke is rated at 92 hp. Am I just a poor drag racer, or is this a reasonable driveline loss? (22 hp, or about 25%) Also, what are other quarter times that anyone has gotten with the 2.5l? mine was 19.4 ish at 70mph ish.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||08-24-2001||04:48 PM||||I'm running a 3.1 and don't even have any official timeslips, but your calculations sound pretty close.  25% driveline loss is also probably not far off.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||08-24-2001||06:56 PM||||Don't forget to add the weight of the driver to the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||08-24-2001||07:50 PM||||Using a vehicle weight of 2700 pounds and HP of 190, *assuming perfect driving*, the quad should run low 14's at 96-97 mph.  Using these formulas:<P>ET = ((Weight / HP)^.333) * 5.825<P>MPH = ((HP / Weight)^.333) * 234<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||08-24-2001||08:40 PM||||I believe most of these formulas are based on rear wheel HP (figure 15-20% drivetrain loss)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Posthumane||08-25-2001||01:27 AM||||Standard, those are the formulas I used. I really would like to improve my times, but I'm wondering if I should try to improve the duke motor or go the engine swap route. I have a very limited budget (student) and this is my daily driver at the moment, but I want to eventually turn it into a GT racer/autocrosser.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pontiacs4me||08-25-2001||03:44 AM||||This is the most accurate hp calculator I have found yet.  It takes two methods and averages them.  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.gordon-glasgow.org/hpcalc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gordon-glasgow.org/hpcalc.html</A> ||||reg||1
